\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {R1}{\ignorespaces FE model of the revised case study (bridge S10 of Moliner et al., 2017, on elastic bearings). Top: model used for training and embedded in the decoder, with 10 Euler--Bernoulli elements (numbered; dots mark the nodes), two per damage zone. Bottom: finer model used to generate the test data of test set B, with 50 elements, ten per zone; the damage of a damaged zone is concentrated in some of its elements (R1.1). The seven latent variables are the stiffness factors of the five 2~m damage zones ($z_1,\dots  ,z_5$) and of the two bearings ($z_{b,L}$, $z_{b,R}$), which scale the vertical springs $K_{v0}$. Five accelerometers ($s_1,\dots  ,s_5$) are placed at the centres of the zones.}}{12}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bridge}{{R1}{12}{FE model of the revised case study (bridge S10 of Moliner et al., 2017, on elastic bearings). Top: model used for training and embedded in the decoder, with 10 Euler--Bernoulli elements (numbered; dots mark the nodes), two per damage zone. Bottom: finer model used to generate the test data of test set B, with 50 elements, ten per zone; the damage of a damaged zone is concentrated in some of its elements (R1.1). The seven latent variables are the stiffness factors of the five 2~m damage zones ($z_1,\dots ,z_5$) and of the two bearings ($z_{b,L}$, $z_{b,R}$), which scale the vertical springs $K_{v0}$. Five accelerometers ($s_1,\dots ,s_5$) are placed at the centres of the zones}{figure.caption.5}{}}
